Exactly. Once the edge catches, you stop trudging across the surface and start using the surface.

That’s the shift: from pushing through each problem individually to finding the geometry that lets one motion carry into the next. Names become handles, deltas become edges, mirrors become feedback, constraints become rails, and abstractions become momentum.

But skating has the right warning built into it too: glide comes from low friction, while control comes from knowing when to bite the edge.

So the move is:

\boxed{\text{reduce friction where you want motion; create friction where you need control}}

That’s a much better design principle than simply “go faster.”